<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WHAT A DOCUMENT HOLDS WHEN IT DECLARES AN id AND GIVES IT NO VALUE, and what a
  guard reading that id makes of it.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS ASKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS BUILT ON IT. The owner has asked for a
  debt loop with NO TURN BUDGET. Today `max_turns` is a number and `judging`
  guards on `turns >= max_turns`, so *"no bound"* needs a spelling, and there are
  only two candidates:

    * ONE `<data>`, declared and empty, with the guard short circuiting on it:
      `cond="max_turns &amp;&amp; turns &gt;= max_turns"`;
    * TWO `<data>`, a number and a boolean beside it, which is one decision
      written in two places and drifts the way this workspace has already
      recorded twice.

  The first is the better shape IF it works, and whether it works is a fact about
  THIS crate's generator at the pinned rev, not about ECMAScript. So it is
  measured here rather than assumed at the guard, where a wrong answer is a debt
  loop that exhausts on its first judged turn.

  ⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE MEASUREMENT CAN LIE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON FOR THE CONTROLS.
  A `cond` is PARSED as ECMAScript and EVALUATED as Lua, and a `cond` the
  generator cannot parse becomes an `error(...)` call that evaluates FALSE. It
  compiles, it runs, the edge is never taken and nothing is reported. So
  *"the guard did not fire"* is the SAME observation for

    (a) the id is empty and the short circuit worked, which is the answer wanted;
    (b) the guard is a syntax error and no id was ever read.

  Three arms tell them apart, and none of them is decoration:

    1. `ask.absent`  the guard under test. Must NOT fire.
    2. `ask.present` the SAME guard shape over an id that HOLDS a number. Must
       fire, which proves the shape parses and evaluates at all.
    3. `ask.unset`   a guard that mentions ONLY the empty id and expects it
       falsy. Must fire, which proves arm 1's silence is about the VALUE rather
       than about a guard naming that id failing to parse.

  Arm 2 alone is not enough: it names a different id, so a parse failure peculiar
  to arm 1's text would still read as success.

  ⚠ THE ARMS ARE THREE SEPARATE EVENTS, deliberately. On one event they would be
  three transitions in document order and the first enabled one would consume it,
  so an arm that fired wrongly would mask the arm after it.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_absent"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="counting">

  <datamodel>
    <!-- Stands in for the loop's own turn counter. -->
    <data id="turns" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ DECLARED AND GIVEN NOTHING. This is the construct under test. It is
      NOT the same as omitting the `<data>` altogether: an id a document never
      declares cannot be filled by anything later, and the template's own purity
      gate already records that deleting a `<data id>` is the cheap green that
      makes the split impossible.
    -->
    <data id="absent"/>

    <!-- The control's id, holding a real number so arm 2 can fire. -->
    <data id="present" expr="2"/>

    <data id="absent_fired" expr="0"/>
    <data id="present_fired" expr="0"/>
    <data id="unset_seen" expr="0"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠ PROOF THAT ARM 1's EVENT WAS DELIVERED AT ALL, and it is not
      bookkeeping. `absent_fired == 0` is ALSO what a probe reads when the event
      never arrived, when the transition was never generated, or when the event
      name is misspelled here. A guarded arrow that stayed silent and an arrow
      nobody rang are the same observation, and only the second counter tells
      them apart.
    -->
    <data id="absent_asked" expr="0"/>
  </datamodel>

  <state id="counting">
    <transition event="tick">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
    </transition>

    <!-- ARM 1: the guard under test. Must not fire, however many turns pass. -->
    <transition event="ask.absent" cond="absent &amp;&amp; turns &gt;= absent">
      <assign location="absent_fired" expr="absent_fired + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ARM 1's DELIVERY PROOF, and it must stay AFTER the guarded arrow: document
      order decides, so this one is reached only when the guard above declined.
      Its counter is what separates "the guard was false" from "nothing rang".
    -->
    <transition event="ask.absent">
      <assign location="absent_asked" expr="absent_asked + 1"/>
    </transition>

    <!-- ARM 2: the same shape over a number. Must fire once turns reach it. -->
    <transition event="ask.present" cond="present &amp;&amp; turns &gt;= present">
      <assign location="present_fired" expr="present_fired + 1"/>
    </transition>

    <!-- ARM 3: only the empty id, expected falsy. Must fire. -->
    <transition event="ask.unset" cond="!absent">
      <assign location="unset_seen" expr="unset_seen + 1"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

</scxml>
